import type {Module} from './evidence';

type GlossaryDepth={sections:Module[];faqs:{q:string;a:string}[];related:{title:string;href:string}[]};
const section=(id:string,label:string,title:string,text:string,refs:string[]=[]):Module=>({id,label,title,paragraphs:[{text,refs}]});

export const glossaryDepth:Record<string,GlossaryDepth>={
  'absolute-risk':{
    sections:[
      section('probability','Meaning','A probability needs a population and a period','Absolute risk describes how many people in a defined population experience an outcome during a defined interval. Its denominator is people at risk, rather than only the people who develop the outcome. A lifetime probability, a one-year probability and the proportion diagnosed during a cohort’s follow-up answer different questions. The age distribution and diagnosis also belong in the description: a probability for all brain tumours in older women cannot stand in for childhood leukaemia.', ['million-women','ahlbom']),
      section('ratio','Example','Why a relative estimate is insufficient','Ahlbom’s pooled analysis reported a relative risk of 2.00 in its highest residential magnetic-field category. That number compares groups; it does not say that two children in every hundred will develop leukaemia. Translating a relative comparison into an absolute difference requires an appropriate baseline risk and time window. The exposed case and control counts in a case-control dataset do not themselves provide that population baseline, because the sampling design selects participants by outcome.', ['ahlbom']),
      section('follow-up','Interpretation','Keep follow-up attached to the number','COSMOS followed adults for brain-tumour diagnoses and reported hazard ratios for call-time comparisons. A hazard ratio alone does not supply the cumulative probability of a tumour for an individual. A useful absolute-risk account would show the baseline event probability, follow-up duration and corresponding uncertainty for a relevant population. It would also explain whether participants were still observed throughout that interval, rather than treating the study’s total enrolment as everyone’s denominator at every time point.', ['cosmos'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Can an elevated relative risk still describe an uncommon outcome?',a:'Yes. Relative change and baseline frequency are separate. A large ratio can accompany a small absolute difference when the outcome is rare; the ratio alone cannot quantify that difference.'}],
    related:[{title:'Relative risk',href:'/glossary/relative-risk-rr'},{title:'Hazard ratio',href:'/glossary/hazard-ratio-hr'},{title:'Childhood leukaemia',href:'/outcomes/childhood-leukaemia'}]
  },
  association:{
    sections:[
      section('relationship','Meaning','A relationship between measured variables','An association means that an outcome varies with an exposure measure in the observations being analyzed. It describes a comparison, without identifying the reason for it. The exposure may be reported behavior, modeled dose or a measured field. Those are different variables. In Rahban’s study, more frequent reported phone use was associated with lower total sperm count. The observation concerns phone-use frequency and a semen endpoint; it does not directly measure an RF effect on conception.', ['rahban']),
      section('explanations','Interpretation','Several explanations can fit the same pattern','A causal effect is one possible explanation for an association. Confounding, participant selection, measurement error and chance can also contribute. Draper found an association between birth-address distance from high-voltage lines and childhood leukaemia, extending farther than expected for line-related magnetic fields. That mismatch matters: distance may capture features other than the field of interest. Simply replacing the word “distance” with “magnetic exposure” would add a causal step that the comparison does not establish.', ['draper']),
      section('comparison','Example','Ask what changed and how it was measured','HERMES related estimated RF brain dose to one-year figural-memory change in adolescents. Its prospective sequence helps describe which information came first, but modeled exposure and residual confounding still affect interpretation. A precise account names the memory measure, follow-up and analysis population. It also distinguishes the full-sample interval from a subset result. “Associated with memory change” is therefore a bounded observation, rather than a finding that every wireless technology impairs all forms of cognition.', ['hermes'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a study have to prove causation to be useful?',a:'No. An association can identify a question worth testing and show where results differ. Its usefulness depends on preserving the measured exposure, outcome and plausible alternative explanations.'}],
    related:[{title:'Confounding',href:'/glossary/confounding'},{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Memory and cognition',href:'/outcomes/cognition'}]
  },
  bias:{
    sections:[
      section('systematic-error','Meaning','Error with a direction in the research process','Bias is systematic distortion arising from how a study recruits people, measures variables, follows participants or analyzes observations. It differs from random sampling variation. Increasing sample size can narrow a confidence interval while leaving a biased comparison intact. The direction is not always obvious: a process may raise an estimate, lower it or affect categories differently. Calling a result biased requires identifying a plausible mechanism of distortion, rather than merely disagreeing with its conclusion.', ['interphone','mobi-kids']),
      section('recall','Example','Recall and participation can alter a comparison','INTERPHONE compared recalled phone use in people with brain tumours and controls. Remembering use after diagnosis can differ between groups, and people who participate may have different phone habits from those who decline. These issues are particularly relevant to its highest recalled call-time category, where elevated glioma odds and implausible use reports both need to remain visible. They complicate interpretation of that estimate; they do not justify silently deleting it or declaring a known correction factor.', ['interphone']),
      section('precision','Interpretation','A confidence interval does not repair bias','The residential magnetic-field pooled analyses report uncertainty intervals around elevated estimates in uncommon high-exposure groups. Those intervals address statistical uncertainty under their analytical assumptions. They do not automatically include the possible effect of selective participation. Assessing bias therefore requires information about the study process as well as the numerical interval. It is possible for a result to be statistically precise yet systematically distorted, or relatively imprecise without a clearly identified large bias.', ['ahlbom','greenland'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does “possible bias” mean the result is false?',a:'No. It identifies an alternative explanation or distortion to assess. The evidence must support its likely direction and importance before treating it as a complete explanation.'}],
    related:[{title:'Confidence interval',href:'/glossary/confidence-interval'},{title:'Case-control study',href:'/glossary/case-control-study'},{title:'Exposure misclassification',href:'/glossary/exposure-misclassification'}]
  },
  'case-control-study':{
    sections:[
      section('sampling','Meaning','Start with the outcome, then compare exposure','A case-control study selects people who have the outcome and a comparison group intended to represent the population that produced those cases. Investigators then compare exposure histories. This approach can study uncommon diagnoses without following an enormous population until sufficient events occur. The control group’s role is crucial: controls are not simply people without symptoms, but a sample whose exposure distribution should represent the relevant source population.', ['interphone','mobi-kids']),
      section('exposure','Example','Historical exposure can be reconstructed in different ways','INTERPHONE asked about past phone use, making recall after diagnosis part of the exposure assessment. Draper instead used birth-address distance from high-voltage lines, avoiding that particular reliance on remembered phone habits. These are both case-control designs, but their measurement problems differ. An address-based proxy may still misrepresent actual magnetic exposure, while a questionnaire may be affected by inaccurate recall. The design label alone does not determine which exposure estimate is more relevant.', ['interphone','draper']),
      section('estimate','Interpretation','Read the sampling design alongside the odds ratio','Case-control results are commonly expressed as odds ratios. The sampled proportion of cases is set partly by recruitment, so it is not an estimate of how common the disease is in the population. INTERPHONE’s elevated odds in the highest recalled-use category describe a particular comparison, subject to participation and recall limitations. MOBI-Kids addresses a different age range and tumour group. Neither comparison automatically supplies lifetime risk or a result for every wireless exposure.', ['interphone','mobi-kids'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Can a case-control study establish that exposure came first?',a:'It can investigate exposure before diagnosis, but timing must be reconstructed accurately. The choice of exposure window and the quality of historical information determine how convincing that sequence is.'}],
    related:[{title:'Odds ratio',href:'/glossary/odds-ratio-or'},{title:'Cohort study',href:'/glossary/cohort-study'},{title:'Brain tumours',href:'/outcomes/brain-tumours'}]
  },
  'cohort-study':{
    sections:[
      section('follow','Meaning','Follow a population through time','A cohort study identifies a population and relates exposure information to outcomes observed over time. A prospective study collects relevant information before subsequent outcomes occur. This sequence can reduce reliance on remembering exposure after diagnosis. It does not randomly assign exposure or guarantee comparable groups. People with different phone habits may also differ in age, occupation or other characteristics, so follow-up and appropriate adjustment are integral to interpreting the comparison.', ['cosmos','million-women']),
      section('diagnoses','Example','The cohort question depends on the endpoint','COSMOS and the Million Women Study followed participants for brain-tumour diagnoses and found no increased risk under their studied conditions. Their populations, use categories and follow-up differ. HERMES is also prospective, but investigates adolescent memory change over one year using estimated brain dose. Calling all three “cohorts” describes a broad study structure. It does not make their outcomes interchangeable or turn one year of memory testing into long-term cancer surveillance.', ['cosmos','million-women','hermes']),
      section('time','Interpretation','Who remains under observation matters','A cohort estimate depends on when people enter follow-up, how exposure is measured and how events and departures are recorded. Baseline phone-use categories may not capture later changes in devices or habits. Rare tumour subtypes can remain imprecise even in a large cohort. The most useful reading pairs the estimate with its actual exposure contrast, population and follow-up, then asks whether those observations address the exposure history and latency relevant to the reader’s question.', ['cosmos','million-women'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does prospective mean experimental?',a:'No. Prospective describes the timing of observation. A cohort can follow people forward without assigning their exposure, leaving confounding and exposure measurement as important considerations.'}],
    related:[{title:'Latency',href:'/glossary/latency'},{title:'Hazard ratio',href:'/glossary/hazard-ratio-hr'},{title:'Mobile-phone evidence',href:'/sources/mobile-phones'}]
  },
  'confidence-interval':{
    sections:[
      section('uncertainty','Meaning','Uncertainty around an estimated comparison','A confidence interval is calculated by a method with specified statistical assumptions. It conveys uncertainty around an estimate, rather than all uncertainty about the scientific question. For a ratio measure, 1 represents no difference in that comparison; for a difference in scores, 0 is the corresponding reference. The interval’s width helps show precision. Its interpretation must retain the units and scale, because a hazard-ratio interval and a memory-score interval describe different quantities.', ['cosmos','hermes']),
      section('memory','Example','Read the estimate and its interval together','HERMES reported a full-sample figural-memory change estimate of −0.22 points per 953 mJ/kg/day dose interquartile range, with a 95% interval from −0.47 to 0.03. The interval includes zero; the point estimate is still negative. This is compatible with several values under that model, rather than proof of exactly no difference. Its operator-data subset used a different dose interquartile range and had a different interval, so both the scale and population matter.', ['hermes']),
      section('bias','Interpretation','Precision and validity are separate questions','COSMOS reported a glioma hazard ratio of 1.07 with a 95% interval of 0.62–1.86 in a high call-time comparison. Reporting only the central estimate hides considerable statistical uncertainty. Conversely, a narrow interval would not eliminate systematic exposure error or confounding. An interval is also not a boundary between biologically possible and impossible effects. It should be read with the study design, event counts and the assumptions that produced the numerical estimate.', ['cosmos'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does crossing the no-difference value prove no effect?',a:'No. It means that the interval includes that value. The range may also include effects in either direction, whose practical importance depends on the outcome and scale.'}],
    related:[{title:'Statistical significance',href:'/glossary/statistical-significance'},{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Memory and cognition',href:'/outcomes/cognition'}]
  },
  confounding:{
    sections:[
      section('third-factor','Meaning','A third factor can mix two relationships','Confounding occurs when another factor is related to both the exposure comparison and the outcome, distorting the association attributed to exposure. The factor must be considered in the causal structure; any variable correlated with exposure is not automatically a confounder. In phone-use studies, reported behavior may travel with other personal or social characteristics. The scientific question is whether those characteristics could explain part of the observed outcome difference, rather than merely whether the groups look different.', ['rahban','hermes']),
      section('proxy','Example','A location can represent more than a field','Draper compared childhood cancer outcomes by birth-address distance from high-voltage lines. The leukaemia association extended farther than expected for line-related magnetic fields, making chance and confounding relevant explanations. A distance category could capture aspects of residence other than the magnetic quantity being investigated. This does not identify a particular alternative cause. It shows why an association with a geographic proxy is insufficient by itself to attribute the difference to the physical exposure.', ['draper']),
      section('adjustment','Interpretation','Adjustment depends on what was measured','Statistical adjustment can compare groups after accounting for recorded factors, but it cannot perfectly remove influences that were unmeasured or measured poorly. HERMES retains residual confounding as a limitation despite its prospective design. Rahban’s cross-sectional phone-habit comparison also cannot establish cause from an adjusted association alone. A useful result therefore names the measured endpoint and comparison and explains remaining uncertainty; the word “adjusted” does not make an observational study equivalent to randomized exposure assignment.', ['hermes','rahban'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Is every alternative explanation confounding?',a:'No. Recall error, selective participation and random variation are different mechanisms. Naming the mechanism matters because each requires different evidence and different methods to assess or reduce it.'}],
    related:[{title:'Association',href:'/glossary/association'},{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Power-line evidence',href:'/sources/power-lines'}]
  },
  dosimetry:{
    sections:[
      section('quantity','Meaning','Describe the exposure that reaches the system','Dosimetry measures or estimates exposure relevant to a body, tissue or experimental preparation. It connects the source’s output to the quantity experienced by the system being studied. Frequency, geometry, duration and the measured quantity all matter. An electric-field strength in V/m, magnetic flux density in μT and absorbed RF power in W/kg describe different aspects of exposure. They cannot be ranked on one numerical scale by comparing the printed numbers alone.', ['icnirp','lf']),
      section('model','Example','Behavior is an input rather than a dose','HERMES estimated RF brain dose using a model, whereas INTERPHONE used recalled phone use and COSMOS analyzed call-time estimates. Each approach contains information about exposure, but hours of use are not absorbed energy per unit mass. A model must connect behavior and devices to the relevant tissue quantity. An error in the behavioral input or in that connection can affect which participants are assigned higher estimated dose, even when the arithmetic is internally consistent.', ['hermes','interphone','cosmos']),
      section('comparison','Interpretation','Match tissue, time and experimental geometry','De Iuliis exposed purified sperm outside the body and reported SAR in W/kg. NTP studied whole-body RF exposure in rats. Although both involve absorbed power, the biological system, exposure schedule and distribution differ. Dosimetry helps articulate these differences; it does not by itself provide a conversion from a laboratory result to an individual’s disease probability. Comparing experiments requires the actual exposure protocol and tissue context, including temperature conditions, alongside the biological endpoint.', ['de-iuliis','ntp-rats'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Can distance alone tell me the dose?',a:'No. Distance is one part of exposure geometry. Source operation, frequency, body position and the quantity of interest are also needed before estimating a relevant dose.'}],
    related:[{title:'SAR',href:'/glossary/sar'},{title:'Power density',href:'/glossary/power-density'},{title:'Exposure misclassification',href:'/glossary/exposure-misclassification'}]
  },
  'double-blind':{
    sections:[
      section('concealment','Meaning','Keep the assigned condition concealed','Double-blind describes a design in which participants and relevant study personnel do not know the assigned condition during testing. The practical question is who was blinded, when and to which information. Concealment can reduce changes in reporting, behavior or assessment driven by expectations about exposure. It is particularly useful for symptoms that participants report themselves. Blinding does not make symptoms unreal; it helps separate the assigned field condition from what participants believe is happening.', ['eltiti','headache']),
      section('provocation','Example','Open-label and blinded comparisons differ','Eltiti tested GSM/UMTS-like base-station signals against sham in people reporting sensitivity and controls. Open-label symptom reports differed from the blinded findings; blinded tests did not show increased symptom number or severity attributable to the tested signals. The comparison illustrates why knowledge of exposure can matter. It does not establish that expectation explains every participant’s symptoms, and it does not turn a short laboratory session into a test of long-term residential exposure.', ['eltiti']),
      section('limits','Interpretation','Blinding addresses one part of study validity','The phone-headache experiment used double-blind sham control, but its selected sample was small and its exposure window was acute. The 5G sleep experiment also used double-blind sham control, yet its genotype-specific EEG endpoint requires its own interpretation. Blinding helps protect the assigned-condition comparison; it does not expand the sample, validate every endpoint or establish clinical importance. A successful reading keeps concealment, randomization, exposure conditions and outcome measurement as separate design features.', ['headache','5g-sleep'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Are randomized and double-blind the same?',a:'No. Randomization concerns how conditions are assigned. Blinding concerns who knows those assignments. A study can use one feature without successfully implementing the other.'}],
    related:[{title:'Sham exposure',href:'/glossary/sham-exposure'},{title:'Nocebo effect',href:'/glossary/nocebo-effect'},{title:'Symptom evidence',href:'/outcomes/ehs-symptoms'}]
  },
  eeg:{
    sections:[
      section('recording','Meaning','Electrical activity is a measured endpoint','Electroencephalography records electrical activity associated with the brain. Researchers can analyze the recording for features such as frequency patterns during sleep. A measured feature has a particular scale and physiological interpretation; it is not a general score of brain health. Distinguishing the recording from the proposed clinical meaning is essential. A change can be scientifically interesting without establishing impaired sleep, a neurological diagnosis or a harmful effect on everyday functioning.', ['5g-sleep']),
      section('spindles','Example','The sleep-spindle result has a specific scope','The selected 5G experiment studied genotyped volunteers exposed to specified 700 MHz and 3.6 GHz signals before sleep. A shift in spindle center frequency after 3.6 GHz exposure was observed in one CACNA1C genotype group. That statement identifies a signal, EEG feature and subgroup. Replacing it with “5G damages sleep” would introduce a clinical outcome and a population-wide conclusion that the reported physiological observation does not establish.', ['5g-sleep']),
      section('scales','Interpretation','Separate signal frequency from EEG frequency','The gigahertz frequency describing an RF exposure and the frequency of an EEG feature describe different physical quantities in different systems. They should not be treated as a single frequency being directly read out by the brain recording. Interpretation also requires the exposure timing, sham comparison, analysis population and uncertainty. In this source record, the small sample and unresolved details about registry history and analysis prespecification limit how strongly the subgroup finding can be generalized.', ['5g-sleep'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does an EEG change prove that sleep was worse?',a:'No. That requires evidence connecting the measured feature to an adverse sleep outcome. The reported spindle-frequency shift alone does not establish poor sleep or clinical injury.'}],
    related:[{title:'Frequency',href:'/glossary/frequency'},{title:'Double-blind',href:'/glossary/double-blind'},{title:'Sleep evidence',href:'/outcomes/sleep'}]
  },
  'electric-field':{
    sections:[
      section('voltage','Meaning','Field strength is measured in volts per meter','An electric field is associated with electric charge and voltage. Its strength is commonly expressed in volts per meter, written V/m. That is a field quantity, distinct from magnetic flux density in tesla and from absorbed RF power per unit mass in W/kg. The units identify what has been measured. A report of an electric field therefore cannot be read as a magnetic-field measurement simply because both exposures come from electrical equipment.', ['icnirp','lf']),
      section('rat-experiment','Example','V/m describes a specified experimental field','Ramazzini exposed rats to a 1.8 GHz GSM field at specified electric-field strengths, including sham and several active levels. The highest tested group had an increase in male-rat heart schwannomas. Both the field description and the animal endpoint belong in that account. A value in V/m describes the exposure setup; it is not itself a tumour probability or a direct measurement of the absorbed dose in every organ.', ['ramazzini']),
      section('comparability','Interpretation','Frequency and geometry complete the description','Two fields with the same numerical V/m value can differ in frequency, temporal pattern, orientation and interaction with the body. RF guideline context and low-frequency guideline context therefore need to be kept separate. The childhood-leukaemia pooled analyses focus on residential magnetic fields in μT; their exposure categories are not electric-field limits. Before comparing a meter reading with a research result, match the measured quantity and frequency and establish whether the exposure circumstances are comparable.', ['icnirp','lf','ahlbom'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Is V/m interchangeable with W/kg?',a:'No. V/m describes electric-field strength; W/kg describes absorbed power per unit mass. Relating them requires information about frequency, geometry and the exposed system.'}],
    related:[{title:'Magnetic flux density',href:'/glossary/magnetic-flux-density'},{title:'SAR',href:'/glossary/sar'},{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'}]
  },
  elf:{
    sections:[
      section('power-frequency','Meaning','Power-frequency fields are a specific exposure family','ELF means extremely low frequency. The central examples in these guides are the 50–60 Hz fields associated with electrical power. These need to be distinguished from the radiofrequency signals used by wireless communication devices. A source can produce both electric and magnetic fields, which require different quantities and units. In the residential childhood-leukaemia studies discussed here, the central exposure variable is magnetic flux density, rather than a generic measure of all electromagnetic exposure.', ['lf','ahlbom']),
      section('residential','Example','Read the exposure category in its own units','Ahlbom’s pooled analysis used estimated residential magnetic fields and reported an elevated estimate in the category at or above 0.4 μT. Greenland used a different higher-field comparison. Those category boundaries organize observational data; they are not demonstrated points at which a causal effect begins. The small proportion of highly exposed participants and possible selection bias affect interpretation. A category label should remain attached to the analysis that produced it.', ['ahlbom','greenland']),
      section('distance','Interpretation','A nearby line is not a complete exposure measurement','Draper used birth-address distance from high-voltage lines as a proxy, rather than directly measuring each child’s magnetic exposure. Distance, measured field and wiring-based categories can therefore describe different aspects of the same broad topic. The association extended farther than expected for line-related fields, leaving alternative explanations relevant. These residential observations do not supply a result for RF phone use, nor do RF experiments resolve the causal meaning of the ELF association.', ['draper','greenland'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a 50–60 Hz result apply to a 3.6 GHz signal?',a:'It does not transfer directly. Frequency, field quantity, exposure conditions and endpoint must match before one study can answer a question about another exposure.'}],
    related:[{title:'Frequency',href:'/glossary/frequency'},{title:'Magnetic flux density',href:'/glossary/magnetic-flux-density'},{title:'Power-line evidence',href:'/sources/power-lines'}]
  },
  'exposure-misclassification':{
    sections:[
      section('wrong-category','Meaning','The assigned exposure differs from the relevant exposure','Exposure misclassification occurs when the exposure assigned to a participant does not accurately represent the exposure being investigated. It can arise from inaccurate recall, coarse categories, imperfect measurements or a mismatch between a proxy and the relevant time window. The error may affect everyone similarly or differ by outcome or other characteristics. Its impact is not universally toward no association: the direction depends on the measurement and analytical structure.', ['interphone','cosmos','draper']),
      section('phone-habits','Example','A habit is an imperfect proxy for RF exposure','Rahban measured reported phone-use frequency, while Hatch examined reported phone location and prospective pregnancy outcomes. Two people reporting the same habit need not have the same RF exposure because their devices and operating patterns can differ. Conversely, coarse reporting categories can group unlike exposures together. A comparison based on carrying location should therefore be described as a carrying-location comparison, rather than silently being rewritten as a direct measure of testicular dose.', ['rahban','hatch']),
      section('time-window','Interpretation','Measure the right place and period','A birth-address distance category may not capture later residences or magnetic fields from other sources. A baseline phone questionnaire may not describe every later year of use. COSMOS and the Million Women Study reduce some retrospective recall problems through their cohort structure, but their exposure estimates still have limitations. Improving exposure assessment means aligning the quantity, location and period with the biological question, rather than assuming that a larger sample resolves errors in those assignments.', ['draper','cosmos','million-women'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does an objective measurement eliminate misclassification?',a:'No. It can accurately measure the wrong time, location or quantity for the question. Relevance to the intended exposure matters as well as instrument accuracy.'}],
    related:[{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'},{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Cohort study',href:'/glossary/cohort-study'}]
  },
  fecundability:{
    sections:[
      section('per-cycle','Meaning','The probability of conception in a cycle','Fecundability is the probability of conception during a menstrual cycle among couples at risk of conception. It is a time-to-pregnancy concept, rather than a count of sperm or a single laboratory measurement. Its denominator and observation window differ from those of a semen study. Following couples across cycles can investigate conception probability over time, but the result still depends on how pregnancy attempts, follow-up and relevant participant characteristics are recorded.', ['hatch']),
      section('carrying','Example','Phone carrying and pregnancy follow-up','Hatch’s two preconception cohorts related reported male phone location to prospective pregnancy outcomes. They observed little overall association between front-pocket carrying and fecundability, with a lower estimate in a leaner subgroup. The overall and subgroup comparisons should remain separate. The source record withholds the numerical estimate because the indexed abstract contains a malformed interval pending verification against the original table. Its qualitative finding does not support inventing a precise percentage change.', ['hatch']),
      section('different-endpoints','Interpretation','Semen parameters do not substitute for conception','Rahban studied semen quality, and De Iuliis exposed purified sperm outside the body. Those studies investigate distinct endpoints and biological settings. A sperm-count association or a laboratory motility change is not itself a measured reduction in per-cycle conception probability. Fecundability also reflects a couple’s reproductive circumstances, so a result cannot automatically be attributed entirely to one partner or a particular RF exposure. Report the actual exposure proxy and reproductive endpoint before connecting the studies.', ['rahban','de-iuliis','hatch'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does lower fecundability mean permanent infertility?',a:'No. A lower per-cycle conception probability is not a diagnosis of permanent infertility. The study’s follow-up, population and outcome definition determine what it can establish.'}],
    related:[{title:'Cohort study',href:'/glossary/cohort-study'},{title:'In vitro',href:'/glossary/in-vitro'},{title:'Male fertility evidence',href:'/outcomes/male-fertility'}]
  },
  frequency:{
    sections:[
      section('cycles','Meaning','Cycles per second identify the field’s frequency','Frequency is the number of cycles per second, measured in hertz, or Hz. Kilohertz, megahertz and gigahertz represent thousands, millions and billions of cycles per second. Electrical-power examples in these guides center on 50–60 Hz; wireless experiments use much higher frequencies. Frequency describes the field’s oscillation, rather than its strength, duration or absorbed dose. A frequency label alone therefore cannot determine how intense an exposure was or what biological outcome was observed.', ['lf','icnirp']),
      section('signals','Example','Different signals remain different experiments','The selected sleep study used 700 MHz and 3.6 GHz signals before sleep, while De Iuliis used 1.8 GHz in an isolated-sperm preparation. These share the broad RF category but differ in exposure setup and endpoint. NTP’s rat experiments used 900 MHz signals with GSM and CDMA modulation. Keeping the actual frequency and signal description attached to each result prevents a finding from becoming a claim about every device marketed under a broad technology name.', ['5g-sleep','de-iuliis','ntp-rats']),
      section('two-frequencies','Interpretation','An exposure frequency differs from a physiological frequency','A sleep-spindle center frequency is a feature extracted from a brain recording; a 3.6 GHz carrier frequency describes the applied RF signal. Both use the idea of cycles per second, but they belong to different measurements. The sleep experiment’s subgroup observation does not mean the EEG simply reproduced the RF carrier. For comparisons across studies, pair frequency with field quantity, dose distribution, exposure timing and the measured biological endpoint.', ['5g-sleep','icnirp'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a higher frequency automatically mean a larger dose?',a:'No. Frequency and dose describe different properties. Estimating absorbed exposure also requires field intensity, duration, geometry and the properties of the exposed system.'}],
    related:[{title:'Radiofrequency',href:'/glossary/radiofrequency-rf'},{title:'ELF',href:'/glossary/elf'},{title:'EEG',href:'/glossary/eeg'}]
  },
  hazard:{
    sections:[
      section('potential','Meaning','Potential for harm under specified conditions','Hazard concerns whether an agent or situation can cause harm under some conditions. Risk concerns the probability of that harm in a specified population and exposure setting. Evidence that supports a hazard question may come from several streams, including human observations and animal experiments. The identification does not itself provide an individual probability, a universal threshold or a prediction for every exposure level. The conditions and endpoint remain part of the claim.', ['iarc','ntp-rats']),
      section('classification','Example','A classification is not an everyday probability','IARC classified RF fields as possibly carcinogenic in 2011. That hazard classification summarizes the evidence concerning carcinogenic potential; it is not a statement that a particular phone user has a specified chance of cancer. Subsequent studies add observations with their own designs. COSMOS did not show increased brain-tumour risk under its studied conditions, while NTP reported particular rat tumour signals. These findings contribute different information and should not be reduced to votes on an individual probability.', ['iarc','cosmos','ntp-rats']),
      section('translation','Interpretation','An animal finding needs a separate human-risk argument','Ramazzini’s increase in male-rat heart schwannomas at its highest tested exposure is relevant to a hazard discussion. Converting it into a numerical human phone-use risk would require a validated connection across species, exposure distribution, schedule and endpoint. A hazard statement can remain scientifically consequential while that quantitative connection is unresolved. Likewise, a cohort’s result for particular brain-tumour diagnoses does not answer every possible hazard associated with every RF exposure.', ['ramazzini','cosmos'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a hazard label tell me how likely harm is?',a:'No. A probability estimate needs a defined exposure, population, endpoint and period. Hazard identification addresses a different part of that assessment.'}],
    related:[{title:'Absolute risk',href:'/glossary/absolute-risk'},{title:'In vitro',href:'/glossary/in-vitro'},{title:'Animal cancer evidence',href:'/outcomes/animal-cancer'}]
  },
  'hazard-ratio-hr':{
    sections:[
      section('event-rate','Meaning','Compare event rates during follow-up','A hazard ratio compares event rates among people still under observation and still able to experience the event, under a survival model. The hazard describes the event rate at a point in follow-up; it differs from the cumulative probability of having experienced the event by the end. A ratio of 1 is the no-difference reference for that modeled comparison. Interpretation depends on the exposure categories, adjustment and the model’s assumptions about how rates behave over time.', ['cosmos','million-women']),
      section('cosmos','Example','A glioma estimate with considerable uncertainty','COSMOS reported a glioma HR of 1.07, with a 95% confidence interval of 0.62–1.86, for its high cumulative call-time comparison. The central estimate is near 1, while the interval shows the limited precision of that comparison. It does not mean a 7% absolute probability of a tumour, nor does it identify the exact effect for every user. The call-time contrast, follow-up and diagnosis are necessary parts of the result.', ['cosmos']),
      section('labels','Interpretation','Check the method as well as the summary label','The Million Women Study’s public record preserves the authors’ reported RR terminology while noting that its Methods specifies Cox hazard ratios. This is a useful reminder that headlines and abstracts may use “risk” broadly. The statistical method determines the precise estimand. Neither an HR nor a reported RR alone supplies the baseline event probability needed for absolute risk. Follow-up duration, participant population and the interval should accompany any interpretation of the numerical ratio.', ['million-women'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Is HR 2 the same as twice the lifetime risk?',a:'No. It compares modeled event rates during follow-up. The relationship to cumulative risk depends on baseline rates, time and model assumptions; lifetime risk is a separate quantity.'}],
    related:[{title:'Relative risk',href:'/glossary/relative-risk-rr'},{title:'Absolute risk',href:'/glossary/absolute-risk'},{title:'Cohort study',href:'/glossary/cohort-study'}]
  },
  'in-vitro':{
    sections:[
      section('outside-organism','Meaning','Study a preparation outside the living organism','In vitro research is conducted outside a living organism, for example in isolated cells or a prepared tissue sample. It allows investigators to control exposure and examine specific biological processes. The preparation has its own conditions, including medium, temperature and experimental handling. Calling cells “human” identifies their origin; it does not make an experiment a trial in living people. The absence of intact-body physiology is part of the model, rather than a minor detail.', ['de-iuliis']),
      section('sperm-experiment','Example','A cellular finding has a cellular scope','De Iuliis exposed purified human sperm to 1.8 GHz RF and reported oxidative stress, DNA fragmentation and reduced motility and vitality with increasing exposure. These are distinct cellular endpoints. The study does not directly observe pregnancy, infertility diagnoses or cancer in people carrying phones. Its 2013 correction replaces Figure 1D and specifies biological replicates, so the correction belongs alongside the result whenever the experiment is discussed.', ['de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction']),
      section('translation','Interpretation','Connect the mechanism to an intact-person outcome','A controlled cellular effect can support investigation of a mechanism. To connect it to everyday exposure requires additional steps: relevant absorbed dose, exposure schedule, intact-body conditions and a link from the assay endpoint to the clinical outcome. Hatch’s conception follow-up and Rahban’s semen observations address different steps with different limitations. Agreement in broad reproductive vocabulary is insufficient to treat these as repeated measurements of the same effect or as a direct estimate of individual fertility risk.', ['de-iuliis','hatch','rahban'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Should an in vitro result be dismissed?',a:'No. It can be useful mechanistic evidence. Its value is clearest when the preparation, exposure, assay and proposed connection to a human outcome are stated explicitly.'}],
    related:[{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'},{title:'Fecundability',href:'/glossary/fecundability'},{title:'DNA-damage evidence',href:'/outcomes/dna-damage'}]
  },
  'ionizing-radiation':{
    sections:[
      section('photon-energy','Meaning','Ionization refers to removing electrons','Ionizing radiation has sufficient photon energy to remove electrons from atoms. X-rays are an example. Power-frequency fields and the RF signals used in the wireless studies presented here are non-ionizing. This distinction concerns the physical interaction, rather than a general judgment that every exposure is harmless or harmful. The word “radiation” covers different phenomena, so the actual frequency and energy regime must be identified before comparing biological mechanisms.', ['icnirp','lf']),
      section('mechanism','Interpretation','A cellular endpoint does not identify the physical route','The De Iuliis experiment reported DNA fragmentation and oxidative stress in isolated sperm exposed to RF. Observing a DNA-related endpoint does not turn that RF exposure into ionizing radiation. The physical classification and the measured cellular response are separate statements. Interpreting a proposed mechanism requires the actual exposure setup and biological pathway, rather than assuming that every form of DNA damage must arise through the electron-removal mechanism associated with ionizing photons.', ['de-iuliis']),
      section('exposure-level','Example','Non-ionizing still needs an exposure description','RF exposure guidance considers quantities such as absorbed power, while low-frequency guidance addresses a different frequency range and interaction context. Thus “non-ionizing” is a useful starting distinction, but it is not a complete dose assessment. NTP’s controlled whole-body rat exposure and a human phone-use cohort can both concern non-ionizing RF while asking different biological questions. Their findings need their species, endpoint, dose distribution and schedule to remain visible.', ['icnirp','lf','ntp-rats','cosmos'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does non-ionizing mean no biological effects are possible?',a:'No. It identifies an energy regime. Whether an exposure produces an adverse outcome requires evidence about its intensity, duration, biological system and measured effect.'}],
    related:[{title:'Frequency',href:'/glossary/frequency'},{title:'Radiofrequency',href:'/glossary/radiofrequency-rf'},{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'}]
  },
  latency:{
    sections:[
      section('interval','Meaning','The interval between exposure and detectable outcome','Latency is the time between an exposure of interest and a detectable outcome. The relevant interval depends on the proposed process and on what counts as detection. A symptom measured during a laboratory session and a tumour diagnosed years later have different timing questions. Follow-up begins at a study-defined point, which may occur well after exposure started. The duration of observation and the duration of prior use therefore should not be treated as the same quantity.', ['headache','cosmos']),
      section('cohort','Example','Follow-up limits the time window actually observed','COSMOS analyzed adult phone use with a median follow-up of 7.12 years. That describes the cohort’s observation interval, rather than proving that every participant’s exposure began seven years before diagnosis. Its public record identifies longer latency as a reason for continued follow-up. The Million Women Study contributes a different population and longer reported follow-up. Combining their lessons requires keeping exposure history, age and tumour subtype visible rather than choosing the larger time label alone.', ['cosmos','million-women']),
      section('acute','Interpretation','A short provocation study answers an acute question','The double-blind phone-headache experiment compared pain and discomfort during short RF and sham exposures. It can test immediate symptom triggering under those conditions, but its design cannot observe a disease that would emerge only after years. Conversely, a cancer cohort is not a controlled test of symptoms during a particular exposure session. Matching the observation window to the proposed outcome is essential before calling findings reassuring, concerning or contradictory.', ['headache','cosmos'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does longer follow-up automatically solve latency?',a:'No. Investigators still need relevant exposure histories and sufficient events in the appropriate window. Longer observation can help while leaving measurement error, population limits and rare endpoints unresolved.'}],
    related:[{title:'Cohort study',href:'/glossary/cohort-study'},{title:'Exposure misclassification',href:'/glossary/exposure-misclassification'},{title:'Brain-tumour evidence',href:'/outcomes/brain-tumours'}]
  },
  'magnetic-flux-density':{
    sections:[
      section('tesla','Meaning','A magnetic quantity expressed in tesla','Magnetic flux density is a measure of the magnetic field, expressed in tesla, or T. Residential power-frequency studies often use microtesla, μT, or milligauss, mG. One μT equals 10 mG. This is a unit conversion for the same quantity, rather than a biological conversion or safety rule. Magnetic flux density differs from electric-field strength in V/m and absorbed RF power in W/kg; the numbers cannot be compared as if their units were interchangeable.', ['lf','icnirp']),
      section('category','Example','Convert a unit without changing the result','Ahlbom’s highest category began at 0.4 μT, which is 4 mG. Writing that boundary in another unit leaves the analysis unchanged. It remains an observational exposure category associated with an elevated estimate, based on relatively few highly exposed participants. It does not become a proven threshold at which harm starts. Greenland used a different category contrast, so comparing the analyses requires their actual boundaries and source-study overlap as well as consistent units.', ['ahlbom','greenland']),
      section('measurement-window','Interpretation','A reading needs a place, time and frequency','A measured magnetic value depends on where and when it is obtained and what the source is doing. A brief reading at one location is not automatically the long-term residential exposure represented in an epidemiological analysis. Draper’s distance categories are another kind of proxy altogether. Interpreting a number therefore requires the frequency range, measurement protocol, time averaging and relevant exposure period. Unit conversion helps readability but cannot supply those missing dimensions.', ['draper','ahlbom'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Is 0.4 μT a guideline limit?',a:'In Ahlbom it is an analytical category boundary. An observational category and an exposure-guideline limit answer different questions and should not be substituted for each other.'}],
    related:[{title:'ELF',href:'/glossary/elf'},{title:'Electric field',href:'/glossary/electric-field'},{title:'Power-line evidence',href:'/sources/power-lines'}]
  },
  'nocebo-effect':{
    sections:[
      section('expectation','Meaning','Negative expectations can influence experienced symptoms','A nocebo effect describes symptoms or adverse responses influenced by negative expectations. The symptoms are real experiences; the term concerns a possible contributor to their occurrence or severity. It does not mean that a participant is inventing symptoms, and it is not a diagnosis that can be assigned from a study headline. Expectation, knowledge of exposure and the assigned physical exposure are separate variables that a well-designed provocation study tries to distinguish.', ['eltiti','headache']),
      section('blinding','Example','Concealed exposure helps separate expectation from assignment','Eltiti compared specified base-station-like signals with sham in people reporting sensitivity and controls. Open-label symptom reports differed from the blinded results, while blinded testing did not show a symptom increase attributable to the tested signals. This makes expectation relevant to interpreting the comparison. It does not establish the mechanism of every symptom, explain every withdrawal or provide a general explanation for all symptoms experienced around electrical equipment outside the laboratory.', ['eltiti']),
      section('attribution','Interpretation','A sham response is informative without being a diagnosis','The phone-headache study found no significant difference in pain and discomfort between real RF and sham conditions in its selected sample. Symptoms during sham show why symptoms and verified field assignment should be recorded separately. However, an individual’s response in one session can have multiple influences. The experiment tests acute triggering by the specified signal, rather than diagnosing all alternative causes or settling questions about delayed symptoms and long-term residential exposure.', ['headache','eltiti'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does discussing nocebo dismiss symptoms?',a:'It should not. The term addresses one possible influence on real symptoms. A group experiment cannot identify every contributor to an individual’s experience or establish a personal diagnosis.'}],
    related:[{title:'Double-blind',href:'/glossary/double-blind'},{title:'Sham exposure',href:'/glossary/sham-exposure'},{title:'Symptom evidence',href:'/outcomes/ehs-symptoms'}]
  },
  'odds-ratio-or':{
    sections:[
      section('odds','Meaning','Odds and probability have different denominators','Odds compare the probability of an event with the probability of no event. An odds ratio compares those odds between groups. It is therefore different from a risk ratio, which directly compares event probabilities. Odds ratios are commonly reported in case-control studies, where investigators sample cases and controls rather than following a representative population to measure event probabilities. The fraction of cases in that sampled dataset is not the population’s absolute disease risk.', ['interphone','greenland']),
      section('interphone','Example','Keep the highest-use odds comparison intact','INTERPHONE reported a glioma OR of 1.40, with a 95% interval of 1.03–1.89, in its highest recalled call-time category. That compares odds for a particular exposure contrast; it is not a 40% absolute chance of glioma. The overall results and the highest-category finding both belong in the account. Recall error, selective participation and implausible use reports affect causal interpretation even though the interval in that comparison lies above 1.', ['interphone']),
      section('rare-outcomes','Interpretation','Approximation is conditional, not a change of label','For uncommon outcomes, an odds ratio can be numerically close to a risk ratio under appropriate conditions. That does not make the concepts identical, particularly when outcomes are common. Greenland’s pooled childhood-leukaemia result is reported as an OR, whereas Ahlbom’s record uses RR. Preserving those labels avoids implying that the analyses calculated exactly the same quantity. Their category definitions and overlapping underlying studies also matter before treating the estimates as independent confirmations.', ['greenland','ahlbom'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Can I turn an odds ratio into an absolute risk directly?',a:'No. You need an appropriate baseline probability and a valid interpretation for the design and population. The ratio alone does not contain the required baseline risk.'}],
    related:[{title:'Relative risk',href:'/glossary/relative-risk-rr'},{title:'Case-control study',href:'/glossary/case-control-study'},{title:'Absolute risk',href:'/glossary/absolute-risk'}]
  },
  'pooled-analysis':{
    sections:[
      section('combine-data','Meaning','Combine data while preserving their origins','A pooled analysis combines data from several studies into a joint analysis. Individual-participant pooling can use original records, harmonize variables and apply common analytical definitions. It differs from a summary-estimate meta-analysis that combines published study-level estimates. Both approaches depend on the quality, compatibility and availability of their inputs. More records can improve precision, but combining them does not automatically remove selection bias, exposure error or differences in source-study methods.', ['ahlbom','greenland']),
      section('leukaemia','Example','Two pools can ask related but different questions','Ahlbom pooled individual records from nine studies of residential magnetic fields and childhood leukaemia. Greenland used original individual data from fifteen studies, including measured fields and wiring-based proxies. Both reported elevated estimates in relatively uncommon higher-field categories, but they used different contrasts and statistical labels. These details matter more than calling both results simply “positive.” Neither pool turns its analytical category boundary into a demonstrated biological threshold.', ['ahlbom','greenland']),
      section('overlap','Interpretation','Do not count shared participants twice','The two pooled analyses overlap in their underlying studies. They should not be counted as two entirely independent new populations, added to all their component studies as if each contributed separate participants. A useful synthesis identifies which records are shared and what new analytical question each pool addresses. Agreement can still be informative, but its interpretation depends on the independence of the evidence and on whether the same measurement or participation limitations recur across analyses.', ['ahlbom','greenland'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a pooled analysis always outweigh a single study?',a:'No. Its value depends on relevant compatible data, analytical choices and source quality. A larger combined sample can retain shared biases or conceal important differences between studies.'}],
    related:[{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Relative risk',href:'/glossary/relative-risk-rr'},{title:'Childhood leukaemia',href:'/outcomes/childhood-leukaemia'}]
  },
  'power-density':{
    sections:[
      section('area','Meaning','Power crossing an area is an external exposure quantity','Power density expresses power passing through a unit area, commonly watts per square meter, W/m², in RF measurements. It describes a different quantity from the absorbed power per unit mass represented by SAR. The area in the denominator is not a body mass or an exposure duration. A useful report identifies frequency, location, time averaging and measurement geometry, rather than treating a single power-density number as a complete account of tissue exposure.', ['icnirp']),
      section('external-internal','Interpretation','External fields and absorbed dose are connected by a model','A field outside the body and energy absorbed within tissue are related through the exposure conditions and the properties of the exposed system. Converting between power density, electric-field strength and SAR is therefore not a universal substitution of units. The RF guideline context distinguishes exposure quantities for different circumstances. A comparison must use the appropriate assumptions about geometry and frequency, especially when moving between a device close to the body and an environmental field.', ['icnirp']),
      section('study-context','Example','The reported metric belongs to the experiment','Ramazzini described specified electric-field strengths for its rat exposure, while De Iuliis reported SAR in an isolated-sperm preparation. A reading in W/m² should not be placed beside those numbers as if a larger printed value meant a larger biological dose. First identify whether the study reports an incident field or absorbed quantity and what was exposed. Even an appropriate physical conversion would not convert the animal or cell endpoint into a human clinical risk estimate.', ['ramazzini','de-iuliis'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a power-density reading tell me the dose in an organ?',a:'Not by itself. That requires relevant exposure geometry, frequency and absorption information. The external measurement and the internal absorbed quantity should remain clearly identified.'}],
    related:[{title:'SAR',href:'/glossary/sar'},{title:'Electric field',href:'/glossary/electric-field'},{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'}]
  },
  'radiofrequency-rf':{
    sections:[
      section('spectrum','Meaning','A broad frequency family, not one exposure','Radiofrequency refers to a region of the electromagnetic spectrum used for wireless communication and other applications. The exact frequency range and definition should be checked in the relevant source. A broad RF label does not specify intensity, modulation, duration or absorbed dose. Phones, base-station-like laboratory signals and other wireless systems can share that label while exposing different body regions under different conditions. Each result therefore needs the actual signal and exposure description.', ['icnirp','eltiti']),
      section('examples','Example','Keep the experiments’ frequencies and endpoints distinct','NTP studied 900 MHz whole-body exposure in rats. De Iuliis exposed purified sperm to 1.8 GHz RF. The sleep experiment used specified 700 MHz and 3.6 GHz signals in genotyped volunteers. These are distinct animal, cell and human observations. A common RF category does not make a rat tumour, sperm assay and sleep-spindle shift equivalent outcomes, nor does it make their dose distributions and exposure schedules interchangeable.', ['ntp-rats','de-iuliis','5g-sleep']),
      section('human-studies','Interpretation','Technology names do not supply a matched study','Human phone-use cohorts often analyze reported or calibrated use patterns rather than directly measured dose for every tissue. Their findings answer those exposure comparisons and diagnoses. Likewise, a base-station provocation experiment tests specified short-term signals against sham. Transferring any of these results to a different wireless technology requires an explicit argument about exposure and endpoint similarity. The word “RF” alone supplies neither a universal safety verdict nor a universal hazard magnitude.', ['cosmos','million-women','eltiti'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Can a phone study answer every Wi-Fi question?',a:'No. A shared RF category is insufficient. The frequency, operating conditions, exposure quantity, population and endpoint need to match the question being asked.'}],
    related:[{title:'Frequency',href:'/glossary/frequency'},{title:'SAR',href:'/glossary/sar'},{title:'Mobile phones',href:'/sources/mobile-phones'}]
  },
  'relative-risk-rr':{
    sections:[
      section('ratio','Meaning','Compare outcome probabilities between groups','Relative risk is the probability of an outcome in one group divided by the probability in a comparison group over a specified period. A value of 1 is the no-difference reference; a value of 2 means twice the comparison risk. The denominator group is essential to the meaning. A ratio describes relative magnitude, while the number of additional events depends on baseline frequency. It does not by itself establish that exposure caused the difference.', ['ahlbom','draper']),
      section('pooled-result','Example','Twice the comparison risk is not a 2% risk','Ahlbom reported a summary RR of 2.00, with a 95% interval of 1.27–3.13, for residential magnetic fields at or above 0.4 μT. That is a relative comparison in an observational pooled analysis. It is not an absolute probability for an exposed child and does not identify a proven safety boundary. Few participants were in the higher category, and selection bias and other explanations remain relevant to causal interpretation.', ['ahlbom']),
      section('estimand','Interpretation','Preserve the reported measure and comparison','Draper’s record reports an RR for birth-address distance categories, which is a proxy comparison rather than a measured magnetic-dose response. The Million Women Study uses RR terminology in reporting while specifying Cox hazard ratios in its Methods. These examples show why “risk” in ordinary language is not always enough to identify the statistical measure. Retain the authors’ estimate, explain the method and keep odds ratios, hazard ratios and cumulative risks conceptually distinct.', ['draper','million-women'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does RR below 1 prove protection?',a:'No. It may reflect chance, confounding, selection or measurement issues as well as a causal effect. The interval, design and exposure comparison determine the interpretation.'}],
    related:[{title:'Absolute risk',href:'/glossary/absolute-risk'},{title:'Odds ratio',href:'/glossary/odds-ratio-or'},{title:'Hazard ratio',href:'/glossary/hazard-ratio-hr'}]
  },
  sar:{
    sections:[
      section('absorbed-power','Meaning','Absorbed power divided by mass','Specific absorption rate, or SAR, is absorbed electromagnetic power per unit mass, expressed in watts per kilogram, W/kg. It describes a rate of energy absorption, rather than total energy accumulated over an unspecified period. A complete description includes whether the value is local or whole-body averaged, how it is spatially averaged and over what time. Values using different averaging conventions cannot be compared reliably by their numerical size alone.', ['icnirp']),
      section('cell-preparation','Example','An isolated-cell SAR belongs to that preparation','De Iuliis reported a SAR range of 0.4–27.5 W/kg in purified human sperm exposed to 1.8 GHz RF. The experiment found changes in cellular endpoints, including DNA fragmentation and motility. Those values describe its experimental setup. They do not directly specify the dose to reproductive tissue during ordinary phone carrying. The preparation, temperature conditions, exposure duration and biological setting matter when asking how the laboratory exposure relates to an intact person.', ['de-iuliis','de-iuliis-correction']),
      section('whole-body','Interpretation','Local and whole-body exposure answer different questions','NTP’s rat study used whole-body RF exposure over a long experimental schedule. Phone-use studies, in contrast, may estimate call time or modeled brain dose rather than assign whole-body SAR. The shared RF category does not make these equivalent exposure measures. A SAR comparison can help characterize absorbed power, but it is not a conversion rule from animal tumour findings to human disease probability. The endpoint and exposure distribution must remain alongside the dose metric.', ['ntp-rats','cosmos','hermes'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Is SAR the same as a W/m² meter reading?',a:'No. SAR measures absorbed power per mass; W/m² describes power per area. Connecting them requires a suitable absorption model and the actual exposure conditions.'}],
    related:[{title:'Power density',href:'/glossary/power-density'},{title:'Dosimetry',href:'/glossary/dosimetry'},{title:'In vitro',href:'/glossary/in-vitro'}]
  },
  'sham-exposure':{
    sections:[
      section('control','Meaning','A comparison condition that resembles active exposure','Sham exposure is designed to resemble the active experimental condition while omitting the field being tested. Participants undergo comparable procedures, timing and surroundings, so the assigned field is the intended difference. “Sham” refers to that specific experimental exposure; it does not claim that the room contains no background fields or that participants experience nothing. The control’s usefulness depends on whether cues such as equipment behavior reveal which condition is assigned.', ['headache','eltiti']),
      section('headache-example','Example','Symptoms can be recorded in both conditions','The phone-headache experiment compared pain and discomfort during paired RF and sham exposures in people reporting phone-related headaches. Symptoms occurred in the experiment, but the comparison did not show a significant difference attributable to active RF. The sham condition makes that distinction possible: symptom occurrence alone is different from a difference between assigned conditions. The small, selected sample and acute testing window remain part of what the experiment can establish.', ['headache']),
      section('design','Interpretation','Sham, blinding and randomization work together','A sham condition supports concealment, while randomization concerns the assignment sequence. The 5G sleep experiment used double-blind sham control to compare sleep-EEG features after specified signals. Its genotype-specific spindle-frequency finding remains a physiological endpoint, despite that controlled design. A sham-controlled result can be informative about the tested signal and time window without answering every chronic-exposure question. Both the fidelity of the sham and the relevance of the measured endpoint matter.', ['5g-sleep','eltiti'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Does a symptom during sham mean it is imaginary?',a:'No. It means the symptom occurred without the active field under test. That observation helps assess attribution while leaving the symptom and its other possible contributors real.'}],
    related:[{title:'Double-blind',href:'/glossary/double-blind'},{title:'Nocebo effect',href:'/glossary/nocebo-effect'},{title:'Headache evidence',href:'/outcomes/headache'}]
  },
  'statistical-significance':{
    sections:[
      section('criterion','Meaning','A criterion within a statistical model','Statistical significance means that a result meets a chosen statistical criterion under a model. It does not measure the size of an effect, its clinical importance or the probability that a causal claim is true. The result depends on sample size, variability, analytical choices and assumptions. A small difference can meet a criterion in a large dataset, while a potentially important difference can remain imprecise in a small study.', ['headache','hermes']),
      section('nonsignificant','Example','A nonsignificant result is not exact equality','The headache experiment reported pain and discomfort increases of 10.1 during RF and 12.6 during sham, with P = 0.30. The study did not find a significant active-versus-sham difference. That is not proof that the true difference is exactly zero or that every form of headache has been tested. The numerical comparison, selected participant group and short exposure window explain the result more usefully than a binary “significant” or “not significant” label alone.', ['headache']),
      section('significant','Interpretation','A significant result still needs a scientific interpretation','INTERPHONE’s highest recalled-use glioma interval excluded 1, yet recall and participation bias still complicated causality. Ramazzini reported a significant increase in male-rat heart schwannomas at its highest exposure, but that does not provide a numerical human risk estimate. The 5G study’s subgroup EEG observation similarly requires endpoint-specific interpretation. Statistical criteria help characterize a comparison; study design, multiple analyses, biological setting and practical meaning determine what claim the result can support.', ['interphone','ramazzini','5g-sleep'])
    ],
    faqs:[{q:'Should only significant findings be reported?',a:'No. Estimates and uncertainty across relevant analyses matter. Selecting only findings that meet a criterion can hide the overall pattern and overstate how decisive the evidence is.'}],
    related:[{title:'Confidence interval',href:'/glossary/confidence-interval'},{title:'Bias',href:'/glossary/bias'},{title:'Association',href:'/glossary/association'}]
  },
};
